=begin pod

=head1 Red::Driver::DuckDB

DuckDB backend for the Raku L<Red ORM|https://github.com/FCO/Red>, built on
L<DB::DuckDB|https://raku.land/zef:skyter10086/DB::DuckDB>.

=head2 Installation

Requires Raku 6.d and DB::DuckDB 1.0.1, whose native binding targets DuckDB 2.x.
Alien::Libduckdb installs the native library automatically. On macOS, first run
C<raku tools/install-native-deps.raku macos>: Alien 1.1.0 downloads the correct archive
but omits C<.dylib> from its library lookup. This helper installs that exact
release with only the missing macOS filenames corrected.
On Windows, run C<raku tools/install-native-deps.raku windows> before installing the
driver dependencies. It extracts the runtime files of DB::DuckDB 1.0.1 to bypass
the zef tar extraction failure on Unicode documentation filenames, then installs
that unchanged runtime. Both helpers use the native C<curl> and C<tar> utilities.

=begin code :lang<sh>

git clone https://github.com/FCO/Red.git .red
git -C .red checkout 589fc491a8544b0d9c2f0a086431256a71322689
git -C .red apply ../tools/red-companion.patch
zef install --/test .red
zef install --/test --deps-only .
zef install .

=end code

The companion changes are currently required; installing an arbitrary published
Red release does not reproduce the schema/migration behavior tested here.
See L<TESTING.md|TESTING.md> for full validation commands.

=head2 Usage

=begin code :lang<raku>

use Red;
my $*RED-DB = database 'DuckDB', :database('example.duckdb');

model Reading {
    has Int $.id is serial;
    has Str $.sensor is column;
    has Rat $.value is column;
}

schema(Reading).create;
Reading.^create(:sensor<temperature>, :value(21.5));
say Reading.^all.grep(*.value > 20).map(*.sensor);

=end code

Omit C<:database> for an isolated in-memory database. An existing C<DB::DuckDB>
object can be supplied as C<:dbh>; the driver retains it and opens a connection
on that database. Statements and transaction wrappers retain that connection.
C<.close> closes the driver connection; it leaves the shared C<dbh> owned by the caller.
For Red's environment-based test configuration use C<RED_DATABASE='DuckDB'> or
C<RED_DATABASE='DuckDB database=/path/to/data.duckdb'>.

Supported: model CRUD with C<RETURNING>, sequence-backed serial IDs, relationships,
foreign keys, joins, grouping, ordering, pagination, primary and unique constraints,
ordinary ART indexes, schema catalog inspection, and begin/commit/rollback.
Types include integers, booleans, strings, floating point, C<DECIMAL(38,18)> for
C<Rat>, dates, UTC timestamps, blobs, UUID and JSON values. Decimals bind as text
to avoid DB::DuckDB's conversion of rational parameters to double precision.
Values outside the declared decimal range/scale remain subject to DuckDB's rules.

=head2 Limits

DuckDB does not support savepoints; C<.supports-savepoints> returns C<False>. Nested Red transactions are rejected rather
than emulated. Cyclic foreign-key schemas are rejected before schema creation;
foreign keys are declared inline and dependencies created first. Schema removal
uses the optional C<Red::Schema> backend hook added in the sibling Red checkout
to drop child tables before their referenced parents. Serial sequences
remain after dropping tables, so recreating a table does not reset its counter.

Row-level locks are explicitly rejected before SQL execution. Partial indexes, PostgreSQL operator classes and exclusion constraints are not
supported. Structured regex predicates use native RE2 or a recursive SQL automaton, including
lookarounds and backreferences. Whole-match text is supported for native patterns
and fixed-width automaton spans; scalar capture text and unsupported Match-boundary
reads remain explicit errors. JSON supports path reads, updates, deletion, presence, element counts and recursive
containment, including dynamic keys/indices. Backend-specific SQL functions are
available through C<.execute>. Migration support requires the companion changes
in the sibling Red checkout: sequence planning, transactional execution and a
separate connection holding a database-scoped migration lock. NOT NULL tightening
uses transactional table reconstruction; defaults, constraints, indexes and
comments are preserved. Tables with generated columns require explicit SQL, and
native dependencies can prevent reconstruction. Temporary database tests transfer
file ownership to child processes because DuckDB permits one writer process.
Native database errors retain their cause;
uniqueness and existing-table errors map to Red's corresponding exceptions.

=head2 Tests

The current complete Red run passes for declared capabilities: 167 files,
2,161 top-level tests, with 15 native-savepoint scenarios explicitly skipped.
The driver suite passes 150 tests, with another 36 tests covering the Raku tools.
See L<TESTING.md|TESTING.md> for the matching
Red checkout and companion patch needed to reproduce these results. CI installs
that patched source; the changes are not yet published as a Red release.


=begin code :lang<sh>

prove6 -Ilib t
# With a sibling Red checkout:
RED_DATABASE=DuckDB prove6 -I../Red/lib -Ilib t ../Red/t/035-create.rakutest

=end code

CI runs the driver tests on Linux, macOS and Windows. Local validation is on macOS;
CI outcomes on the other platforms must be checked separately.

=head2 Backend choice

DB::DuckDB offers the connection/statement/result interfaces needed by Red and
manages native library installation. Duckie is also a general DuckDB binding,
while Duck::CSV is a CSV reader rather than an ORM backend. The driver uses only
DB::DuckDB; CSV, Parquet, Arrow and appender-specific APIs are outside its scope.

